WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.030
Experts on energy, including oil and gas,

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.270
say that solar and wind are
the energy of the future.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.610
But while consumers wait for

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.080
the future expansion
of alternative energy,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.240
the fossil fuel industry
is pushing for natural gas

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.180
to be a bridge to that future.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.310
In the US, drillers are busily tapping gas

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.850
as well as oil, from
underground rocks known as shale

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.440
in a process called hydraulic fracturing,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.533
more commonly known as fracking.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.270
But not everyone is convinced
the transitional fossil fuel

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.120
is all it's cracked up to be.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.100
Despite its benefits,
fracking remains unpopular

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.710
in many parts of the world,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.830
especially among climate activists

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.440
and locals most affected by the process

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.530
and with plummeting oil prices
triggered by the coronavirus

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.900
and Saudi Arabia's stand-off with Russia,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.103
the financial viability
of shale is in jeopardy.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.720
Fracking is a fairly new process
of natural gas extraction.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.440
First commercially introduced in 1949,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.420
it involves forcing water
mixed with sand and chemicals

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.410
into a well to create
fissures in shale rock,

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.960
so oil and gas trapped inside can escape.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.580
Advances in the 1980s

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.790
in a technique called horizontal drilling

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.330
opened up access to thin layers of shale

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.470
even deeper underground.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.280
Over the past decade,
US oil and gas output

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.470
has surged 57%, mostly
due to shale-rich areas,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.210
such as the Permian Basin,

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.783
which stretches from Texas to New Mexico.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.760
America's recent energy dominance

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.730
was meant to undercut Opex
ability to manipulate the market,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.200
but in March of 2020,
Russia triggered an oil war

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.550
with Saudi Arabia,
cratering global prices.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.280
The shale industry, which
has higher production costs

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.400
in places like Saudi Arabia and Russia

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.380
and relies heavily on debt
finance, was hit the hardest.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.860
Major shale operations,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.560
like Whiting Petroleum
Corp and Diamondback,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.010
all but promised major job
cuts and production halts

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.963
after shares dropped
40 to 45% respectively.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.420
Adding to the recent woes,

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.920
fracking remains highly controversial.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.960
It has become a huge target
for climate activists

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.920
who point to its damaging
effects on the environment.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.590
A handful of European countries

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.780
have effectively banned the practice

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.600
due to its environmental impacts.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.500
Opponents cite an enormous amount

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.310
of water needed in fracking.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.750
Depending on the shale
deposits, it can take anywhere

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.530
between 1.5 and 16 millions
gallons to frack a well.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.150
Large though those numbers seem,

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.410
a study by Duke University calculated that

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:39.948
the water used in fracking
makes up less than 1%

00:02:39.948 --> 00:02:43.310
of total industrial water use nationwide.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.270
There's also evidence that
fracking, and more commonly,

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.140
the pumping of resulting
waste water into wells,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.850
triggers earthquakes.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.100
They've mostly been small,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.500
but one in 2016 in the
oil rich state of Oklahoma

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.910
shook homes and buildings across the state

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.010
for almost an minute

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.730
and measured 5.8 on the Richter Scale.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.170
Having an earthquake right now,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.760
our light's shaking quite a bit here

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.070
and it's now calming down

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.140
as another earthquake has just hit.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.810
Tremors in China's shale hub

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.220
in Sichuan Province killed two people

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.910
and damaged 11000 homes
in February of 2019.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:19.220
There's no easy answer on fracking.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.730
No one can deny that shale

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.180
has benefited the US economically,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.740
but now there are very real concerns about

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.640
whether the fracking revolution
is coming to a close.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.580
Defenders who continue to
point to big-picture benefits,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.640
like job creation and energy independence,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.340
are now staring down
the barrel of job cuts,

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.060
scale backs and bankruptcies.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.650
Fracking opponents point to

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.610
the climate warming
methane in natural gas that

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.010
sometimes escapes into the
atmosphere before it gets burned,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.130
and reject industry claims that shale

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.270
is a bridge fuel to a renewable future.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.050
Now faced with an increasingly
hostile economic environment,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.260
in addition to longstanding opposition

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.890
from environmental groups,

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.483
the US shale industry faces
a very uncertain future.

